virglrenderer: Venus says when the driver made no pipelines

What: when the driver's vkCreateGraphicsPipelines or vkCreateComputePipelines returns an
error (vkr_pipelines_made), one virgl_warn line names the context, the app, the call, the
pipeline count and the VkResult (the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th ... failure in the process).
Nothing else changes: the handles stay unknown to vkr, as before.
Why: the guest makes pipelines without waiting and binds the handle; vkr has no object for
it and the bind ends the context with "failed to look up object N of type 19", at info
level, which the runtime's log level (warning) drops. Zed's crash on MoltenVK
(virgl-venus-spirv-inline-array-calls.patch) left only "venus context N (zed-editor) is
lost" in qemu.log; the reason took VIRGL_LOG_LEVEL=info and MVK_CONFIG_LOG_LEVEL=4.
Upstream: no.
Tests: none of its own (a driver failure on demand needs a module the driver refuses
after spirv-val passed it; Zed's were the case, and the inline patch fixes them).

diff --git a/src/venus/vkr_pipeline.c b/src/venus/vkr_pipeline.c
--- a/src/venus/vkr_pipeline.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_pipeline.c
@@ -1182,6 +1182,18 @@ vkr_pipelines_made(struct vkr_context *ctx,
 {
    vkr_unpin(ctx, pins);
    if (ret < VK_SUCCESS) {
+      /* Pingouin (virgl-venus-pipeline-driver-failure.patch): the guest makes pipelines
+       * without waiting and binds the handles; vkr has no object for them, so the bind ends
+       * the context ("failed to look up object N of type 19") with no reason in the log.
+       * The 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th ... failure says it here, at the default log level. */
+      static atomic_uint_fast64_t failed;
+      const uint64_t n = atomic_fetch_add(&failed, 1) + 1;
+      if (!(n & (n - 1)))
+         virgl_warn("venus context %u (%s): %s: the driver made none of the %u pipelines "
+                    "(VkResult %d; %" PRIu64 " failed so far); binding one ends the app's "
+                    "Vulkan context\n",
+                    ctx->ctx_id, ctx->debug_name ? ctx->debug_name : "?", call, arr->count,
+                    ret, n);
       memset(out, 0, arr->count * sizeof(*out));
       object_array_fini(arr);
       return false;
